# Exhibits: claim → generator → input

Every figure/table is generated by `sh figtab/make_all.sh` from analysis's result files (`RESULTS_DIR`, default `results_in/`; schema in `figtab/schema.py`, requested in experiment-repo#2). Nothing is typed by hand; a missing or inconsistent input stops the script instead of drawing. Throw-away layout data used to develop the scripts is never committed: nothing in `figures/` or `tables/` exists until it was generated from a real result file. Each exhibit has a caption-ready `.md` next to it (claim, what is shown, how to read it, what it was computed on, read from the drawn rows).

Geometry (`figtab/geometry.json`, ICML 2027): column 3.25 in, full width 6.75 in, minimum in-figure type 7 pt (venue-repo's recommendation; the venue says only "legible"). Include figures at natural size (no `width=` scaling) so the type is not shrunk: single-column ones are ≤ 3.25 in, wide ones (`figure*`) ≤ 6.75 in. Fonts: matplotlib `usetex` with the psnfss Times, Helvetica and Courier packages, so every font in every figure PDF is an embedded Type 1 font (Nimbus Roman, the font of the ICML body); `sh figtab/check_fonts.sh` runs `pdffonts` on each PDF and fails on anything else (last run: all six PDFs Type 1, all embedded, no Type 3). matplotlib's default preambles need cm-super, absent here, hence the pinned families. Appendix tables are `longtable`s: they need single-column mode (`\onecolumn` in a two-column paper); the main-text tables are plain `tabular`s for `table*`. Each figure `.md` states its natural size and the smallest type at that size.

